// Heaven Benchmark — Rain-impact splashes / ripples (Splash_System)

// Part of the weather system.
// Renders rain-impact ripples on near-horizontal ground beneath the
// camera-centered precipitation volume.

// ── Why this is a SCREEN-SPACE fullscreen pass (the 5th, correct approach) ──

// The previous four attempts built discrete 3D ripple QUADS and placed each by
// projecting one per-slot world anchor to a SINGLE screen pixel, sampling the
// depth buffer there, and dropping the quad at that surface. The scene depth
// buffer contains trees, grass blades and distant islands — not just ground —
// so a single probe pixel routinely landed on foliage/grass tips, leaving rings
// floating in mid-air above the real surface. As the camera moved, that one
// projected pixel swept across different surfaces frame-to-frame, so the rings
// jumped, pulsed and popped. A single per-splash screen probe simply cannot
// reliably find "ground" in a foliage-filled scene.

// This material replaces that entirely with the Breath-of-the-Wild
// screen-space rain technique, adapted to the repo's existing godrays / volfog
// depth-sampling infrastructure. It is a FULLSCREEN world-space quad placed in
// front of the camera each frame (exactly like heaven_godrays.mat /
// heaven_volumetric_fog.mat), and it shades EVERY visible pixel:

//   1. Sample the LINEAR scene depth (F, eye-space FORWARD distance in
//      metres) at the Y-flipped UV (LOCKED convention, scene-depth-pipeline
//      — same as godrays / volfog / heat haze / lens flare). Sky /
//      no-geometry pixels read the far clear value (~2000 m) and output zero —
//      no ripple in the sky. A null/unbound texture reads <= 0 and is treated
//      as far.
//   2. Reconstruct the pixel's WORLD-SPACE position from the linear distance
//      using FILAMENT'S OWN getWorldFromClipMatrix() to unproject the view ray
//      (guaranteed consistent with how the scene + depth pre-pass were
//      rendered), then place the surface point at its eye-forward distance along
//      that ray (worldPos = camWorld + rayDir * dist / dot(rayDir, camFwd)).
//      This yields TRUE camera-relative Z-up world space: flat ground
//      reconstructs the SAME +Z up-normal at every camera angle. (The depth is
//      no longer a reversed-Z value fed straight to clip.z — that attachment is
//      gone; only the linear distance along the unprojected ray is used.)
//   3. Reconstruct the surface NORMAL with an EDGE-AWARE multi-tap method
//      (NOT raw dFdx/dFdy, which is noisy on the half-res depth buffer and
//      garbage at silhouettes). Sample depth at the centre and +/-1 half-res
//      texel in X and Y (5 taps), reconstruct each tap's world position, and on
//      each axis pick the neighbour whose depth is CLOSER to the centre — the
//      standard "improved normal from depth" trick (Turánszki / Yuwen Wu).
//      Building the normal from the two NEAR edge vectors avoids reconstructing
//      across a silhouette/foliage discontinuity. n = normalize(cross(hVec,
//      vVec)); the cross product of two screen derivatives of a PLANAR surface
//      points the same way (+Z for flat ground) at ANY viewing angle, so this
//      is foreshortening-invariant by construction.
//   4. GROUND TEST (the realism step), both parts VIEW-ANGLE-INVARIANT:
//        - Upward-facing only: accept where abs(n.z) >= cos(45°) (Z-up). A
//          planar surface reconstructs the SAME up-normal regardless of how
//          foreshortened it is on screen, so flat ground passes at every angle
//          while trunks / walls (n.z ~ 0) fail. The cone is deliberately LOOSER
//          than weather_shader_ref::splash_accepted's nominal 30° because the
//          half-res depth makes the reconstructed normal wobble a few degrees;
//          a literal 30° over-rejected real ground. weather_shader_ref.h is
//          unchanged.
//        - Edge / foliage rejection as a RELATIVE DEPTH-DISCONTINUITY test, NOT
//          an absolute world-step. A planar surface (even fully foreshortened)
//          has depth that varies SMOOTHLY across neighbours — its second
//          difference is ~0 — whereas a foliage/grass/silhouette edge has a
//          depth JUMP, i.e. a large second difference. We measure the relative
//          second difference of LINEAR depth (camera distance) across the taps,
//          |linZ(L)+linZ(R)-2*linZ(C)| / linZ(C), and reject when it exceeds a
//          small threshold. Because it is normalised by linZ(C) it is
//          scale/distance invariant, and because a foreshortened plane is
//          locally linear in depth it stays ~0 at ANY angle — so flat ground
//          passes at grazing angles while real discontinuities are rejected.
//          This replaces the old length(fwidth(worldPos)) world-step test,
//          which scaled with perspective foreshortening: at a grazing angle two
//          adjacent pixels on flat ground map to world points many metres apart,
//          so that test misclassified flat ground as an edge and rejected it
//          (the green-only-when-top-down symptom).
//   5. Tile ripples in ABSOLUTE WORLD XY so they are world-anchored and stable
//      (do NOT slide with the camera). worldFromClip reconstructs a
//      CAMERA-RELATIVE world position, so we add the pushed ABSOLUTE camera
//      world position (cameraPos uniform) to get an absolute world XY:
//      worldAbs = worldPos + cameraPos. As the camera moves by Δ the
//      camera-relative worldPos decreases by Δ while cameraPos increases by Δ,
//      so worldAbs is camera-INVARIANT for a fixed ground point → the ripple
//      cell grid is world-locked. A ripple cell is floor(worldAbs.xy /
//      rippleSpacing). The cell id is hashed for a per-cell spawn phase, a
//      sub-cell jitter for the ripple centre, a SPATIAL spawn-probability gate,
//      and a per-CYCLE temporal spawn gate — both gates scale with rain
//      intensity so the spatial density AND the temporal rate of impacts track
//      intensity; at medium intensity only a sparse scatter of cells
//      ripple, fewer than the visible falling streaks.
//   6. Animate each spawned cell by age = mod(time - cellPhase, period) with the
//      period hard-capped at <= 2.0 s — mirroring weather_shader_ref::splash_age
//      semantics. An expanding ring grows with age and fades out
//      as age → period, so every impact completes and disappears within 2.0 s.
//      ONE ripple per spawn (a discrete impact, not a constant pulsing pair).
//      Ripples are evaluated by scanning the 3x3 world-space neighbourhood of
//      cells around the fragment and accumulating each neighbour cell's ripple,
//      so a ring centred in one cell spills smoothly into adjacent cells instead
//      of being clipped at the cell boundary (no hard seams between impacts).
//   7. Composite a subtle, cool, translucent highlight over the scene (NOT bold
//      white circles). Alpha is modulated by the ground-accept flag, intensity,
//      a soft distance fade, and the ring profile, and output premultiplied for
//      the transparent blender (same convention as volfog / heaven_unlit).

// Correctness: every shaded ripple pixel comes from a REAL visible depth sample
// that passed the upward-normal (45° visual cone) AND relative depth-discontinuity
// (foliage/edge) tests — both VIEW-ANGLE-INVARIANT — so ripples can only appear
// on visible, near-horizontal, non-foliage ground at ALL viewing angles, never
// on trees / grass / walls. Because the animation is tiled by WORLD XY and is a
// continuous function of world position + time (no discrete quad re-placed per
// frame), the rings are stable in world space and cannot jump / pulse as the
// camera moves. The <= 2.0 s capped age guarantees each ripple completes and
// clears (mirrors splash_age).

// The geometry (the single fullscreen quad) is BUILT ONCE; the splash quad is
// pulled out of the scene during the depth pre-pass
// (RainFeature::on_depth_prepass_begin/end) so it never reads the depth
// attachment it would otherwise be written into — no Vulkan read/write feedback
// hazard.

// Material parameters (pushed per frame by RainFeature::update):
//   depthTexture: sampler2d — shared scene-depth pre-pass (frame.depth_texture)
//   time: float     — global animation time (getUserTime(), seconds)
//   splashIntensity: float     — effective rain intensity in [0,1] (0 => nothing)
//   cameraPos: float3     — ABSOLUTE camera world position (engine Z-up),
//                                        used to recover absolute world XY for the
//                                        world-anchored ripple tiling (Issue 1) and,
//                                        via cameraPos.z, the dropTopZ of the shared
//                                        fall law
//   volumeExtent: float     — camera-centered volume half-extent (matches
//                                        the streak uniform / weather_ref::kVolumeExtent)
//   fallSpeed: float     — nominal fall speed (matches the streak uniform /
//                                        RainFeature kFallSpeed); the splash retimes each
//                                        ripple from the SAME shared fall law the streaks
//                                        use, so a ripple blooms where + when a streak lands

// World reconstruction uses Filament's built-in getWorldFromClipMatrix() inside
// the shader, which yields a CAMERA-RELATIVE world position. The distance fade
// uses the camera-relative camera origin (frameUniforms.worldFromViewMatrix[3].xyz,
// the same value the streak shader uses). The ripple TILING instead needs an
// ABSOLUTE world XY so the cell grid is world-locked, so it adds the pushed
// absolute `cameraPos` uniform (worldAbs = worldPos + cameraPos) — exactly the
// absolute-camera anchoring trick the streak shader (heaven_rain.mat) uses to
// keep drops world-static.

// Ripple tuning (spacing, period, ring width, colour, …) are compile-time
// consts in the fragment shader so the per-frame uniform set stays minimal.

// Compiled with: matc -a opengl -p desktop -o heaven_splash.filamat heaven_splash.mat

material {
    name : heaven_splash,
    requires : [ uv0 ],
    shadingModel : unlit,
    // World-space fullscreen quad (NOT vertexDomain:device), same shell as
    // heaven_volumetric_fog.mat. The CPU places this quad just inside the far
    // plane in front of the camera each frame so every screen pixel runs the
    // fragment shader; the fragment stage does all the work.
    blending : transparent,
    doubleSided : true,
    flipUV : false,
    depthWrite : false,
    depthCulling : false,
    parameters : [
        // Linear scene depth (F, eye-space FORWARD distance in metres) from
        // the depth pre-pass / heaven_depth proxy render (FrameContext::depth_texture).
        { type : sampler2d, name : depthTexture, precision : high, filterable : false },

        // Animation + density.
        { type : float,    name : time },
        { type : float,    name : splashIntensity },

        // ABSOLUTE camera world position (engine Z-up world), pushed per frame
        // from frame.camera->position — exactly the value the streak shader
        // (heaven_rain.mat) pushes and uses to keep drops world-static. This is
        // RE-ADDED to fix the ripples-slide-with-camera bug (Issue 1): the
        // reconstructed worldPos is in Filament's CAMERA-RELATIVE world space
        // (origin near the camera), so worldPos.xy for a FIXED ground point
        // shifts as the camera moves and the ripple cell grid slid with it. We
        // recover an ABSOLUTE world XY for tiling as worldPos + cameraPos: as
        // the camera moves by Δ the camera-relative worldPos decreases by Δ
        // while cameraPos increases by Δ, so the sum is camera-INVARIANT for a
        // fixed ground point → the cell grid is world-locked → ripples stop
        // sliding. (The normal/orientation never needed this because a normal
        // is translation-invariant; only the tiling does.) Used for the XY
        // tiling AND, via cameraPos.z, as the camera Z that anchors the shared
        // fall law's dropTopZ = cameraPos.z + volumeExtent. The distance fade
        // keeps using the camera-relative camWorld.
        { type : float3,   name : cameraPos, precision : high },

        // Camera-centered precipitation volume half-extent (world units) and the
        // nominal fall speed — RE-ADDED for the rain/splash UNIFICATION. The
        // splash now retimes each ripple from the SAME shared lattice + fall law
        // the streak shader uses (engine/weather_shader_ref.h): the impact time
        // of the drop that lands in a ground cell is a closed form in these two
        // values plus the reconstructed ground height, so a ripple blooms
        // exactly where and when a streak comes down. Pushed per frame by
        // RainFeature::update from weather_ref::kVolumeExtent and kFallSpeed —
        // the SAME values the streak uniforms carry, so the two agree.
        { type : float,    name : volumeExtent, precision : high },
        { type : float,    name : fallSpeed,    precision : high },
        { type : float4,   name : splashShapeTuning },
        { type : float4,   name : splashShadingTuning }
    ],
}

vertex {
    // No-op vertex stage. The CPU positions the quad in world space (same as
    // godrays / volfog); we just need UV0 to flow through to the fragment
    // shader.
    void materialVertex(inout MaterialVertexInputs material) {}
}

fragment {
    // Cheap 2D hash → [0,1). Used to derive a per-ripple-cell spawn phase,
    // sub-cell jitter and a spawn-probability gate from the world-space cell id.
    float hash21(float2 p) {
        float3 p3 = fract(float3(p.xyx) * 0.1031);
        p3 += dot(p3, p3.yzx + 33.33);
        return fract((p3.x + p3.y) * p3.z);
    }

    float2 hash22(float2 p) {
        float3 p3 = fract(float3(p.xyx) * float3(0.1031, 0.1030, 0.0973));
        p3 += dot(p3, p3.yzx + 33.33);
        return fract((p3.xx + p3.yz) * p3.zy);
    }

    // One expanding ring at a given age fraction t01 (0..1 within its cycle) at
    // radial distance `r` (world metres) from the ripple centre. The ring front
    // marches outward with age and fades out as age → period, so every impact
    // completes and disappears within the (<= 2.0 s) period.

    // LOOK PASS: the old profile was a symmetric band (equal bright inner +
    // outer edge), which read as a uniform bright stripe. A real water ripple
    // has a bright THIN leading edge at the expanding front and a fainter
    // trailing wash behind it. We now build a sharp leading edge right at the
    // front (narrow smoothstep just inside `front`) and let it decay gently
    // toward the centre (the trailing wash), so the ring reads as water rather
    // than a solid band.
    float rippleRing(float r, float t01, float maxRadius, float ringWidth) {
        float front = t01 * maxRadius;
        // Bright thin leading edge: peaks just inside the marching front and
        // falls off quickly outward (so nothing draws ahead of the front).
        float lead  = smoothstep(front - ringWidth, front, r)
                    * (1.0 - smoothstep(front, front + ringWidth * 0.5, r));
        // Faint trailing wash inside the ring (everything behind the front),
        // decaying toward the centre so the interior is only a soft glint.
        float trail = smoothstep(front - ringWidth * 4.0, front, r) * 0.25;
        float band  = max(lead, trail * (1.0 - smoothstep(front, front + ringWidth, r)));
        // Fade the whole ripple out over its life.
        float fade = 1.0 - t01;
        return band * fade;
    }

    // Reconstruct a world-space position from a screen-space UV and the LINEAR
    // eye-space FORWARD distance (metres) sampled from the linear scene-depth
    // texture (F, produced by the depth pre-pass / heaven_depth proxy render).
    // The depth source is NO LONGER a reversed-Z attachment, so we do not feed a
    // clip-z into getWorldFromClipMatrix; instead we unproject the pixel's view
    // ray with Filament's OWN getWorldFromClipMatrix() (guaranteed consistent
    // with how the scene was rendered) and place the surface point at its
    // eye-forward distance along that ray:
    //     worldPos = camWorld + rayDir * (dist / dot(rayDir, camFwd))
    // The ray is formed by unprojecting a far-plane clip point (Filament
    // reversed-Z far = 0) and subtracting the camera origin; dividing the
    // forward distance by dot(rayDir, camFwd) converts the FORWARD distance into
    // a length along the (possibly off-axis) ray — the same forward-cosine
    // conversion volfog uses (sceneRayLen = sceneDist / cosTheta).

    // camWorld / camFwd are Filament's CAMERA-RELATIVE world camera origin and
    // forward axis, so the result is in the SAME camera-relative Z-up world the
    // old reconstruction produced: flat ground reconstructs the SAME +Z up-normal
    // at every camera angle, and worldAbs = worldPos + cameraPos still recovers
    // the absolute world XY the ripple tiling needs.
    float3 worldFromUV(float2 screenUV, float dist, highp mat4 worldFromClip,
                       float3 camWorld, float3 camFwd) {
        float2 ndcXY   = screenUV * 2.0 - 1.0;
        float4 farClip = float4(ndcXY, 0.0, 1.0);
        float4 wp4     = worldFromClip * farClip;
        float3 worldFar = wp4.xyz / wp4.w;
        float3 rayDir   = normalize(worldFar - camWorld);
        float  cosF     = max(dot(rayDir, camFwd), 1e-3);
        return camWorld + rayDir * (dist / cosF);
    }

    // Sample the linear scene-depth texture (F, eye-space FORWARD distance in
    // metres) at a SCREEN uv. Applies the LOCKED Y-flip (scene-depth-pipeline
    // the offscreen RT is stored top-down versus the quad UV (Vulkan
    // framebuffer orientation), the same convention as heaven_blit / godrays /
    // volfog / heat haze / lens flare. Graceful fallback: a null/unbound
    // texture reads a non-positive value — treat that as "far / no geometry"
    // (2000 m) so splashes are NOT spuriously occluded before the depth pre-pass
    // produces a valid texture.
    float sampleSceneDist(float2 screenUV) {
        float2 dUV = uvToRenderTargetUV(screenUV);
        float d = texture(materialParams_depthTexture, dUV).r;
        return d <= 0.0 ? 2000.0 : d;
    }

    void material(inout MaterialInputs material) {
        prepareMaterial(material);

        // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        // STAGED DEBUG VISUALIZATION (diagnostic only)

        // We cannot see the screen; the USER runs the build and reports what
        // they observe. These modes bisect the splash pipeline so we can find
        // empirically WHERE the pixels are lost. Each mode (except 0) outputs
        // an OPAQUE, unmistakable solid colour (full alpha) and returns
        // immediately, BEFORE the ripple animation. Re-point SPLASH_DEBUG to
        // a different number and re-run `./scripts/build.sh --materials` to
        // switch views.

        //   0 = real shader (no debug)
        //   1 = solid CYAN for EVERY pixel, no tests at all
        //         → confirms the fullscreen quad is actually rendering and is
        //           not culled / occluded / mis-placed. If the screen does NOT
        //           go cyan, the problem is structural, not the shader logic.
        //   2 = sampled LINEAR depth as greyscale (eye-forward metres / 300,
        //         using the same Y-flipped sample the real path uses)
        //         → confirms the depth texture is bound + readable and shows
        //           the on-screen depth range (near ground dark, far/sky bright).
        //   3 = GREEN where the sky test passes (sceneDist < kSkyDist, i.e.
        //         scene geometry), RED where it fails (sky / far plane)
        //         → how much of the screen is "scene" vs "sky" to this pass.
        //   4 = GREEN where sky + upward-normal cone pass, RED otherwise
        //         → isolates the NORMAL test. With the edge-aware multi-tap
        //           reconstruction this should be GREEN on flat ground at ALL
        //           viewing angles, not just top-down.
        //   5 = GREEN where sky + normal + RELATIVE planarity (edge/foliage)
        //         test pass, RED otherwise
        //         → isolates the foliage rejection test (relative 2nd-difference
        //           of linear depth; flat ground ~0 at any angle, edges large).
        //   6 = GREEN where ALL ground tests incl. distance fade pass (every
        //         pixel that COULD host a ripple), RED otherwise
        //         → KEY view: flat ground in front GREEN ⇒ acceptance is fine
        //           and the problem is the ripple gate/profile (tuning); RED
        //           ⇒ acceptance is the culprit and modes 4/5 say which test.
        //   7 = solid YELLOW gated ONLY by splashIntensity > 0.0001 (no other
        //         test); BLACK otherwise
        //         → confirms the splashIntensity uniform reaches the shader as
        //           > 0 during the user's run. If MODE 7 is black, the
        //           intensity uniform is the bug (e.g. effective_intensity()
        //           is 0 in interactive mode).
        //   8 = the reconstructed, edge-aware surface NORMAL encoded as RGB:
        //         baseColor = float4(normalize(n)*0.5 + 0.5, 1). This is the
        //         AXIS-DISAMBIGUATION view. It shows which world axis the
        //         reconstructed ground normal points along, telling us the real
        //         up axis of the reconstructed worldPos space unambiguously:
        //           - flat ground reads BLUE-ish  → n ≈ +Z → space is Z-UP
        //             (matches the code analysis: inverse(perspective*view) with
        //             view = lookAt(.., up=+Z) reconstructs the engine Z-up
        //             world). If so, the abs(n.z) up-test is on the RIGHT axis
        //             and the inversion must come from the normal reconstruction
        //             itself, NOT the up axis.
        //           - flat ground reads GREEN-ish → n ≈ +Y → space is Y-UP and
        //             the up-test must use abs(n.y).
        //           - flat ground reads RED-ish   → n ≈ +X → space is X-up.
        //         (Sky / degenerate pixels read ~grey 0.5,0.5,0.5 since n≈0.)
        //   9 = abs(normal) up-axis probe: baseColor = float4(abs(n.y), 0,
        //         abs(n.z), 1) using the normalized n. One image shows BOTH
        //         candidate up axes at once:
        //           - flat ground BLUE  → abs(n.z)≈1 → Z is up (expected).
        //           - flat ground RED   → abs(n.y)≈1 → Y is up.
        //         A clean grayscale abs(n.z) alone would also be WHITE on flat
        //         ground iff Z is up; the red/blue split just makes the Y-vs-Z
        //         decision a single glance.

        // NOTE: the `intensity <= 0.0001` early-out is bypassed in every debug
        // mode (guarded to MODE 0 only) so the debug colours still show even
        // if the intensity plumbing is the bug.
        // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        #define SPLASH_DEBUG 0

        // MODE 1: solid CYAN for every pixel — NO tests at all. Earliest
        // possible return, before depth is even sampled, so this purely
        // answers "is the quad rendering to the screen?".
        #if SPLASH_DEBUG == 1
            material.baseColor = float4(0.0, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0);
            return;
        #endif

        // Solid debug colours reused by the GREEN/RED pass-fail modes.
        const float4 DBG_GREEN = float4(0.0, 1.0, 0.0, 1.0);
        const float4 DBG_RED   = float4(1.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0);

        // ── Ripple tuning constants (kept off the per-frame uniform set) ──
        // World spacing between ripple cells (metres). Issue 2: raised 0.6 ->
        // 1.2 so each cell covers ~4x the area and far fewer impacts appear per
        // unit ground — the splashes read as clearly separated discrete events,
        // fewer than the visible falling streaks, like real rain.
        const float kRippleSpacing = 1.2;
        // Fraction of cells eligible to ripple at full intensity. Lowered to
        // 0.25 so at medium intensity (0.5) only a sparse scatter of cells ever
        // ripple. This SPATIAL gate scales with rain intensity so splash density
        // tracks intensity; at intensity 0 the shader early-outs entirely
        // so nothing draws. The temporal rate now follows from the shared
        // fall law (how often a drop lands in a cell vs the ripple duration),
        // not a separate per-cycle spawn gate.
        const float kActiveFrac    = 0.25;
        // Ring geometry. With cross-cell spill (the 3x3 world-space scan below)
        // a ripple may now grow up to ~half the spacing and bleed into the
        // neighbouring cells without being clipped, so the max radius is a
        // fraction of the FULL spacing (kept < kRippleSpacing so a cell only
        // ever reaches its immediate neighbours, which the 3x3 scan covers).
        // TUNING: max radius REDUCED 30% per user request (0.5 -> 0.5*0.7 =
        // 0.35 stays well under kRippleSpacing (1.2), so the cross-cell
        // ripple spill still falls within the 3x3 / 5x5 scan neighbourhood
        // (0.35 << 1.2) and rings are never clipped at cell boundaries.
        const float kMaxRadiusFrac = 0.35;
        const float kRingWidth     = 0.12;

        // ── GROUND-TEST constants ────────────────────────────────────────
        // Upward-facing cone half-angle. The reference (weather_shader_ref::
        // splash_accepted) uses a nominal 30° cone, but the SHADER reconstructs
        // its normal from a HALF-RES depth buffer (edge-aware multi-tap, below),
        // which is noisier than an analytic normal: a genuinely flat ground
        // fragment can wobble several degrees off +Z. A literal 30° cone
        // therefore over-rejects real ground. This shader-local visual cone is
        // deliberately LOOSER than the reference's nominal 30° — 45° — so genuine
        // flat ground reliably passes while trunks / walls (n.z ~ 0, ~90° off up)
        // still fail by a wide margin. The KEY point is that the up-normal of a
        // PLANAR surface is the same at every viewing angle, so this test is
        // foreshortening-invariant. weather_shader_ref.h is left untouched; only
        // this visual constant changes. cos(45°) = 0.70710678.
        const float COS_UP_CONE    = 0.70710678;

        // Half-res depth texel size in UV. The depth pre-pass is rendered at
        // w/2 x h/2 (depth_prepass.cpp), so one half-res texel spans 2 screen
        // pixels. We sample +/-1 half-res texel for the multi-tap normal +
        // planarity tests. textureSize() on the bound sampler gives the exact
        // half-res dimensions, so the tap offset is correct at any resolution
        // and we never hardcode a viewport size.
        // (Computed below from textureSize once `depth` is sampled.)

        // Edge / foliage rejection as a RELATIVE second-difference of LINEAR
        // depth (camera distance), NOT an absolute world-step.

        // WHY THE OLD WORLD-STEP TEST KILLED FLAT GROUND AT GRAZING ANGLES: it
        // rejected any pixel where length(fwidth(worldPos)) exceeded an
        // allowance growing with camera distance. But fwidth(worldPos) is the
        // world-space distance between two ADJACENT screen pixels, and under
        // perspective that distance EXPLODES with the viewing angle: looking
        // level/grazing across flat ground, two neighbouring pixels project to
        // world points many metres apart (the ground "stretches" toward the
        // horizon), so length(fwidth(worldPos)) for perfectly flat ground was
        // huge and the test misclassified it as a foliage edge and rejected it.
        // Only top-down (where adjacent pixels stay close in world space) passed
        // — exactly the green-only-when-looking-straight-down symptom.

        // THE FIX is a planarity measure that does NOT depend on viewing angle:
        // the relative second difference of linear depth across the neighbour
        // taps. A planar surface has depth that is locally LINEAR in screen
        // space, so its second difference linZ(L)+linZ(R)-2*linZ(C) is ~0 no
        // matter how foreshortened it is; a foliage/grass/silhouette edge has a
        // depth JUMP, i.e. a large second difference. Normalising by linZ(C)
        // makes it scale/distance invariant:
        //     planarErr = |linZ(L)+linZ(R)-2*linZ(C)| / linZ(C)   (and up/down)
        //     reject if max(planarErrX, planarErrY) > kPlanarityTol
        // For a flat courtyard viewed at 15° above grazing, planarErr ~ 0 (the
        // surface is linear between taps) so it PASSES; a foliage edge has a
        // second difference of order the depth jump itself, planarErr >> tol, so
        // it is rejected — at ANY viewing angle. 0.15 is loose enough for
        // half-res depth quantisation on flat ground, tight enough to catch the
        // metre-scale jumps at foliage/silhouette boundaries.
        const float kPlanarityTol  = 0.15;

        // Soft distance fade so distant ground is not speckled with sub-pixel
        // rings (metres). Pushed out 45/90 -> 60/140 so that in a LEVEL view —
        // where flat ground recedes to mid-distance rather than sitting right
        // under the camera — near AND mid ground in front of the player still
        // host ripples.
        const float kFadeStart     = 60.0;
        const float kFadeEnd       = 140.0;
        // Watery, more saturated blue-cyan highlight. TUNING (user request:
        // "more water-like"): the previous 0.60,0.70,0.82 was a desaturated
        // grey-blue. Pull the RED channel down and keep BLUE highest for a
        // cooler, more chromatic blue-cyan that reads like real water — red
        // lowered, green mid, blue highest. (0.60,0.70,0.82 -> 0.42,0.62,0.82.)
        const float3 kRippleColor  = float3(0.42, 0.62, 0.82);
        // Overall alpha gain. LOOK PASS: dropped 0.9 -> 0.42 so the highlight is
        // markedly more translucent/watery and stops blowing out to solid white.
        // (Combined with the per-cell alpha randomisation below, individual
        // impacts now range from faint to moderate, never opaque.)
        // TUNING: alpha DECREASED a further 10% per user request (0.42 -> 0.42
        // * 0.9 = 0.378) for an even more translucent glint. TUNING: alpha
        // LOWERED a further 20% per user request (0.378 -> 0.378 * 0.8 = 0.3024
        // -> 0.302) for an even more translucent glint.
        const float  kAlphaGain    = 0.302;

        // Open-sky / no-geometry threshold in metres. Geometry reads its
        // (nearer) eye-forward distance; open sky / no geometry reads the far
        // clear value (~2000 m), so a sample at or beyond kSkyDist is "sky" and
        // hosts no ripple — matching the godrays / volfog / SSGI sky cutoff.
        const float  kSkyDist      = 1500.0;

        float intensity = materialParams.splashIntensity;

        // MODE 7: solid YELLOW iff the splashIntensity uniform arrives > 0,
        // BLACK otherwise. No other test. This is the FIRST thing gated by
        // intensity, so it isolates the uniform plumbing: if the user sees
        // black here, effective_intensity() is reaching the shader as 0.
        #if SPLASH_DEBUG == 7
            if (intensity > 0.0001) {
                material.baseColor = float4(1.0, 1.0, 0.0, 1.0);
            } else {
                material.baseColor = float4(0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0);
            }
            return;
        #endif

        // Intensity 0 (e.g. --rain-intensity 0 / --no-rain) ⇒ nothing drawn,
        // Shipping_Look preserved. BYPASSED in debug modes so the debug
        // colours still show even if intensity plumbing is the bug — only the
        // real path (MODE 0) honours this early-out.
        #if SPLASH_DEBUG == 0
        if (intensity <= 0.0001) {
            material.baseColor = float4(0.0);
            return;
        }
        #endif

        float2 uv = getUV0();

        // Sample the LINEAR scene depth (F, eye-space FORWARD distance in
        // metres) produced by the depth pre-pass / heaven_depth proxy render —
        // NOT the old reversed-Z attachment. sampleSceneDist applies the LOCKED
        // Y-flip (offscreen RT is top-down vs the quad UV, same convention as
        // heaven_blit / godrays / volfog / heat haze / lens flare) and the
        // null/unbound guard (reads <= 0 → far 2000 m → treated as sky).
        float sceneDist = sampleSceneDist(uv);

        // Sky test result, reused by the GREEN/RED pass-fail debug modes (4/5/6)
        // which do NOT early-return on sky — they paint sky pixels RED instead.
        // Geometry reads its (nearer) eye-forward distance; open sky / no
        // geometry reads the far clear value (~2000 m) ≥ kSkyDist.
        bool skyPass = (sceneDist < kSkyDist);

        // MODE 2: sampled LINEAR depth as greyscale, normalised by 300 m (near
        // ground dark, far / sky bright), using the SAME Y-flipped sample the
        // real path uses (inside sampleSceneDist). Confirms the depth texture is
        // bound + readable and shows the on-screen depth range.
        #if SPLASH_DEBUG == 2
            float dbgD = clamp(sceneDist / 300.0, 0.0, 1.0);
            material.baseColor = float4(dbgD, dbgD, dbgD, 1.0);
            return;
        #endif

        // MODE 3: GREEN where the sky test passes (scene geometry), RED where
        // it fails (sky / far plane). Shows how much of the screen is "scene"
        // vs "sky" to this pass.
        #if SPLASH_DEBUG == 3
            material.baseColor = (sceneDist < kSkyDist) ? DBG_GREEN : DBG_RED;
            return;
        #endif

        // Sky / far pixels read the far clear value (~2000 m) ≥ kSkyDist. No
        // ripple there. In debug modes 4/5/6 we do NOT early-return here; RED
        // marks the failing pixels so the whole screen stays informative.
        #if SPLASH_DEBUG == 0
        if (sceneDist >= kSkyDist) {
            material.baseColor = float4(0.0);
            return;
        }
        #endif

        // Reconstruct the pixel's world-space position from the LINEAR scene
        // depth (see worldFromUV): unproject the view ray with Filament's own
        // getWorldFromClipMatrix() and place the surface at its eye-forward
        // distance along that ray. camWorld / camFwd are Filament's
        // CAMERA-RELATIVE world camera origin and forward axis (the same values
        // volfog uses), so worldPos is TRUE camera-relative Z-up world: flat
        // ground reconstructs +Z at every camera angle, and worldAbs = worldPos
        // + cameraPos still recovers the absolute world XY the ripple tiling
        // needs. The previous reversed-Z reconstruction (sampled depth fed
        // straight to clip.z) is gone with the depth attachment it read.
        highp mat4 worldFromClip = getWorldFromClipMatrix();
        float3 camWorld  = frameUniforms.worldFromViewMatrix[3].xyz;
        float3 camFwd    = -normalize(frameUniforms.worldFromViewMatrix[2].xyz);
        float3 worldPos  = worldFromUV(uv, sceneDist, worldFromClip, camWorld, camFwd);

        // ── Edge-aware multi-tap depth sampling (5 taps) ─────────────────
        // Sample the linear depth at +/-1 HALF-RES texel in X and Y around the
        // centre. The depth pre-pass is half-res (depth_prepass.cpp renders
        // w/2 x h/2), so textureSize() returns the half-res dimensions and
        // 1/size is exactly one half-res texel in UV — i.e. two screen pixels.
        // Using the real texel size keeps the taps on distinct depth samples at
        // any resolution (raw dFdx/dFdy can stay inside one half-res texel and
        // degenerate).
        float2 texel = 1.0 / float2(textureSize(materialParams_depthTexture, 0));

        // Each neighbour is BOTH sampled and reconstructed at its own SCREEN uv:
        // sampleSceneDist applies the LOCKED Y-flip internally, so the sampled
        // distance and the reconstructed NDC position are the SAME pixel — the
        // reconstructed neighbours of a planar surface are coplanar with the
        // centre, which is what makes the normal view-invariant (the per-tap-
        // consistent mapping proven by depth_reconstruct_ref.h, also mirrored by
        // heaven_ssgi.mat). With the linear depth + Y-flip-in-helper the manual
        // depthUV Y-negation the reversed-Z path needed is gone.
        float2 oL = float2(-texel.x, 0.0);
        float2 oR = float2( texel.x, 0.0);
        float2 oD = float2(0.0, -texel.y);
        float2 oU = float2(0.0,  texel.y);
        float dL = sampleSceneDist(uv + oL);
        float dR = sampleSceneDist(uv + oR);
        float dD = sampleSceneDist(uv + oD);
        float dU = sampleSceneDist(uv + oU);

        float3 wL = worldFromUV(uv + oL, dL, worldFromClip, camWorld, camFwd);
        float3 wR = worldFromUV(uv + oR, dR, worldFromClip, camWorld, camFwd);
        float3 wD = worldFromUV(uv + oD, dD, worldFromClip, camWorld, camFwd);
        float3 wU = worldFromUV(uv + oU, dU, worldFromClip, camWorld, camFwd);

        // Edge-aware normal reconstruction (Turánszki / Yuwen Wu "improved
        // normal from depth"): on each axis pick the neighbour whose depth is
        // CLOSER to the centre depth, so the edge vector never straddles a
        // silhouette/foliage discontinuity (which produced garbage normals
        // before). Build the horizontal + vertical edge vectors from the two
        // chosen NEAR neighbours, then n = cross(hVec, vVec). For a planar
        // surface this cross product points the same way (+Z up for flat ground)
        // regardless of viewing angle, so the up-test is foreshortening-invariant.
        float3 hVec = (abs(dR - sceneDist) < abs(dL - sceneDist))
            ? (wR - worldPos) : (worldPos - wL);
        float3 vVec = (abs(dU - sceneDist) < abs(dD - sceneDist))
            ? (wU - worldPos) : (worldPos - wD);
        float3 n   = cross(hVec, vVec);
        float  nl  = length(n);
        // |dot(n, +Z)| — winding-agnostic up-alignment. A degenerate normal
        // (nl ≈ 0) cannot be shown to face up, so treat it as rejected.
        float  upAlign = (nl > 1e-8) ? abs(n.z) / nl : 0.0;

        // MODE 8 (AXIS DISAMBIGUATION): output the reconstructed, edge-aware
        // surface normal as RGB = normalize(n)*0.5 + 0.5. This is the decisive
        // view for the "which axis is up in the reconstructed worldPos space?"
        // question. Flat ground reads BLUE → n ≈ +Z (space is Z-up, the code
        // analysis prediction; the abs(n.z) up-test is then on the correct axis
        // and any inversion is in the normal reconstruction, not the axis).
        // GREEN → n ≈ +Y (Y-up; up-test must use abs(n.y)). RED → n ≈ +X. A
        // degenerate / sky normal (nl ≈ 0) reads mid-grey (0.5,0.5,0.5).
        #if SPLASH_DEBUG == 8
            float3 nDir8 = (nl > 1e-8) ? (n / nl) : float3(0.0);
            material.baseColor = float4(nDir8 * 0.5 + 0.5, 1.0);
            return;
        #endif

        // MODE 9 (Y-vs-Z up probe): one image encoding both candidate up axes
        // of the normalized normal — red = abs(n.y), blue = abs(n.z). Flat
        // ground BLUE → abs(n.z) ≈ 1 → Z is up (expected). Flat ground RED →
        // abs(n.y) ≈ 1 → Y is up. (Blue channel alone doubles as the grayscale
        // abs(n.z): WHITE-blue on flat ground iff Z is up.)
        #if SPLASH_DEBUG == 9
            float3 nDir9 = (nl > 1e-8) ? (n / nl) : float3(0.0);
            material.baseColor = float4(abs(nDir9.y), 0.0, abs(nDir9.z), 1.0);
            return;
        #endif

        // MODE 4: GREEN where sky + upward-normal cone pass, RED otherwise.
        // Isolates whether the NORMAL test over-rejects. With the edge-aware
        // multi-tap reconstruction this should now be GREEN on flat ground at
        // ALL viewing angles, not just top-down.
        #if SPLASH_DEBUG == 4
            material.baseColor = (skyPass && upAlign >= COS_UP_CONE)
                ? DBG_GREEN : DBG_RED;
            return;
        #endif

        // ── GROUND TEST ──────────────────────────────────────────
        // Upward-facing only (rejects trunks / walls / near-vertical grass).
        // Uses the looser visual cone (45°) — see COS_UP_CONE above. The planar
        // up-normal is the same at every viewing angle, so this is
        // foreshortening-invariant.
        #if SPLASH_DEBUG == 0
        if (upAlign < COS_UP_CONE) {
            material.baseColor = float4(0.0);
            return;
        }
        #endif

        // Camera distance to this ground fragment, used both as the LINEAR depth
        // measure for the planarity test and for the soft distance fade below.
        // worldPos was reconstructed in Filament's CAMERA-RELATIVE world space
        // (see above), so the camera position is taken in that SAME space —
        // Filament's camera world position frameUniforms.worldFromViewMatrix[3]
        // .xyz (already bound above as camWorld), NOT the absolute CPU cameraPos
        // uniform (a different origin), or the distance term would be wrong.
        float camDist  = length(worldPos - camWorld);

        // Edge / foliage rejection as a RELATIVE second difference of LINEAR
        // depth (camera distance) across the neighbour taps. A planar surface is
        // locally linear in depth, so linZ(L)+linZ(R)-2*linZ(C) ≈ 0 at ANY
        // viewing angle (foreshortening cancels in the second difference); a
        // foliage / grass / silhouette edge has a depth JUMP, so the second
        // difference is large. Normalising by the centre distance makes it
        // scale/distance invariant. This replaces the old length(fwidth(worldPos))
        // world-step test, which blew up with foreshortening at grazing angles
        // and rejected flat ground.
        float linC = camDist;
        float linL = length(wL - camWorld);
        float linR = length(wR - camWorld);
        float linD = length(wD - camWorld);
        float linU = length(wU - camWorld);
        float invC = 1.0 / max(linC, 1e-3);
        float planarX = abs(linL + linR - 2.0 * linC) * invC;
        float planarY = abs(linD + linU - 2.0 * linC) * invC;
        float planarErr = max(planarX, planarY);

        // MODE 5: GREEN where sky + normal + relative-planarity (edge/foliage)
        // test pass, RED otherwise. Isolates the foliage rejection test. Flat
        // ground (planarErr ≈ 0) is GREEN at any angle; foliage/silhouette
        // edges (planarErr >> tol) are RED.
        #if SPLASH_DEBUG == 5
            material.baseColor =
                (skyPass && upAlign >= COS_UP_CONE && planarErr <= kPlanarityTol)
                ? DBG_GREEN : DBG_RED;
            return;
        #endif

        #if SPLASH_DEBUG == 0
        if (planarErr > kPlanarityTol) {
            material.baseColor = float4(0.0);
            return;
        }
        #endif

        // Soft distance fade so distant ground isn't speckled.
        float distFade = 1.0 - smoothstep(kFadeStart, kFadeEnd, camDist);

        // MODE 6 (KEY VIEW): GREEN where ALL ground tests incl. distance fade
        // pass — every pixel that COULD host a ripple — RED otherwise. With the
        // foreshortening-invariant tests, flat ground/paths/courtyard in front
        // of the camera at a NORMAL (level / slightly-down) angle must now be
        // GREEN; trees, grass, walls and sky must stay RED.
        #if SPLASH_DEBUG == 6
            material.baseColor =
                (skyPass && upAlign >= COS_UP_CONE && planarErr <= kPlanarityTol
                 && distFade > 0.0)
                ? DBG_GREEN : DBG_RED;
            return;
        #endif

        #if SPLASH_DEBUG == 0
        if (distFade <= 0.0) {
            material.baseColor = float4(0.0);
            return;
        }
        #endif

        // ── World-XY ripple tiling (ABSOLUTE-world-anchored, Issue 1) ─────
        // worldPos is in Filament's CAMERA-RELATIVE world space (origin near
        // the camera), so worldPos.xy for a FIXED ground point shifts as the
        // camera moves — tiling on it slid the cell grid with the camera. We
        // recover an ABSOLUTE world position by adding the pushed absolute
        // camera world position: worldAbs = worldPos + cameraPos. As the camera
        // moves by Δ the camera-relative worldPos decreases by Δ while cameraPos
        // increases by Δ, so worldAbs is camera-INVARIANT for a fixed ground
        // point → the cell grid is world-locked and ripples no longer slide.
        // (The normal never needed this: a normal is translation-invariant; the
        // tiling is NOT.) Only the XY tiling uses worldAbs; camWorld/camDist
        // above stay in camera-relative space for the distance fade.
        float3 worldAbs = worldPos + materialParams.cameraPos;

        // ── Shared WIND constants (mirror weather_shader_ref::kWindDir /
        //    kWindStrength* / kWindGustFreq AND heaven_rain.mat byte-for-byte) ──
        // The splash applies the IDENTICAL wind model the streak uses so a
        // slanted drop splashes exactly where it lands: the ripple centre is the
        // cell's drop column PLUS the wind drift at the moment of impact
        // (windStrength(impactT) * (dropTopZ - groundZ) along kWindDir). At
        // windStrength 0 the drift is exactly zero, so the centre is the column
        // and the no-wind match is unchanged.

        // APPROXIMATION (documented): with wind the drop that LANDS at a given
        // ground XY started from a column OFFSET upwind, so the exact inverse
        // (which lattice cell lands here) is non-trivial. We keep tiling by the
        // GROUND cell and offset each cell's ripple CENTRE by the wind drift at
        // impact. The streak shader uses the IDENTICAL cell + identical wind
        // offset (drop_xy_at_z at z = groundZ), so the streak's landing point and
        // this ripple centre coincide by construction — proven by the extended
        // (prop_rain_splash_match.cpp), which asserts the shared
        // drop_xy_at_z formula equals this centre.
        const float2 kWindDir          = normalize(float2(1.0, 0.4));
        const float  kWindStrengthBase = 0.18;
        const float  kWindStrengthAmp  = 0.005;
        const float  kWindGustFreq     = 0.3;

        // ── Continuous world-space ripple scan, WIND-AWARE (Issue 1 + wind) ──
        // Evaluate ripples in CONTINUOUS world space by scanning a neighbourhood
        // of cells around the fragment and accumulating each neighbour cell's
        // ripple, so a ripple centred in one cell spills smoothly into adjacent
        // cells instead of being clipped at the cell boundary (the hard-seam
        // bug). With WIND each cell's ripple centre is shifted downwind by
        // kWindDir * windStrength(impactT) * (dropTopZ - groundZ): the BASE part
        // of that offset is identical for every cell at this ground height, so
        // we shift the cell LOOKUP by the base offset (recovering the cells whose
        // wind-shifted centres land near the fragment); only the small gust AMP
        // scatters centres per-cell (< kRippleSpacing). A 5x5 scan (±2 cells)
        // covers the base-shifted cell plus that amp residual plus the ripple
        // radius spill (maxRadius < kRippleSpacing), so no impact is clipped.
        float  windBase    = kWindStrengthBase * (materialParams.cameraPos.z + materialParams.volumeExtent - worldAbs.z);
        float2 windBaseOff = kWindDir * windBase;
        float2 baseCell    = floor((worldAbs.xy - windBaseOff) / kRippleSpacing);
        float  maxRadius   = kMaxRadiusFrac * kRippleSpacing;

        // LOOK PASS — per-cell appearance randomisation. Real rain impacts vary
        // in size, brightness and ring width; we derive independent per-cell
        // hashes from the cell id `cid` (DISTINCT seeds so radius/alpha/width do
        // not correlate into a visible pattern) and remap each into a tuning
        // range. Everything stays a pure function of cid, so ripples remain
        // world-stable and frame-rate independent — no per-frame randomness.

        // ── UNIFICATION: impact-driven ripple timing ──
        // The ripple is NO LONGER timed by an arbitrary per-cell spawn phase +
        // cycle gate. Instead each cell is retimed from the SAME shared lattice
        // + fall law the streak shader uses (engine/weather_shader_ref.h): the
        // drop that lands in this ground cell impacts at drop_impact_time(...),
        // and the ripple animates over [impactT, impactT + rippleDuration). So a
        // ripple blooms exactly where AND when a streak comes down (the streak
        // column drop_column_xy(cell) is this cell's ripple centre). The fall
        // span is fixed at 2*volumeExtent and only the per-cell SPEED varies, so
        // the closed-form impact time is exact for ground within the fall band.

        // The shared fall-law constants below mirror weather_shader_ref
        // byte-for-byte (kFallPeriodVary*, the per-cell hash seeds, the ripple
        // duration band). drop_ripple_duration is HARD-CLAMPED to <= 2.0 s so the
        // active ripple window can never exceed the lifetime cap.
        const float kFallPeriodVaryMin = 0.8;
        const float kFallPeriodVaryMax = 1.4;
        float rippleDurMin = materialParams.splashShapeTuning.y;
        // TUNING: minimum ripple expansion speed INCREASED a further 30% per
        // user request. Expansion speed ~= maxRadius / rippleDuration, so the
        // slowest ripples are the longest-duration ones governed by this max.
        // Raising the min speed by another 30% means shortening the longest
        // duration by 1/1.3 again: 1.385 / 1.3 = 1.0654 -> 1.065. Still >
        // kRippleDurMin (0.9) so the mix() band stays valid, and still <= the
        // 2.0  cap, so the cap is unaffected. MIRRORED byte-for-byte in
        // weather_shader_ref.h (kRippleDurationMax) — keep both in lockstep

        float rippleDurMax = materialParams.splashShapeTuning.z;
        const float kRippleDurCap      = 2.0;   // hard lifetime cap

        float  volExtent = materialParams.volumeExtent;
        float  fallSpeed = materialParams.fallSpeed;
        float  dropTopZ  = materialParams.cameraPos.z + volExtent;  // drop_top_z
        float  fallSpan  = 2.0 * volExtent;
        float  groundZ   = worldAbs.z;

        float ring = 0.0;
        float core = 0.0;
        for (int dy = -2; dy <= 2; ++dy) {
            for (int dx = -2; dx <= 2; ++dx) {
                float2 cid = baseCell + float2(float(dx), float(dy));

                // Spatial spawn gate: only a fraction of cells are eligible, the
                // fraction scaling with rain intensity so splash DENSITY tracks
                // intensity; intensity 0 already early-outs. Cells
                // above the gate stay dry this run.
                float gate = hash21(cid + 11.7);
                if (gate > kActiveFrac * intensity) {
                    continue;
                }

                // ── Per-cell randomised appearance (distinct hash seeds) ──
                // Radius scale ~[0.6, 1.3]: impacts come in different sizes.
                float rndR = hash21(cid + 23.1);
                float cellMaxRadius =
                    maxRadius *
                    mix(materialParams.splashShadingTuning.w, 1.3, rndR);
                // Brightness/alpha scale ~[0.5, 1.0]: some impacts faint, some
                // stronger, so the field stops looking uniform.
                float rndA = hash21(cid + 47.9);
                float cellAlpha = mix(0.5, 1.0, rndA);
                // Ring width scale ~[0.7, 1.3]: vary the band thickness a touch.
                float rndW = hash21(cid + 71.3);
                float cellRingWidth = kRingWidth * mix(0.7, 1.3, rndW);

                // ── Shared fall law for this cell (mirrors drop_fall_period /
                //    drop_fall_speed / drop_phase byte-for-byte) ──
                float periodVary = mix(kFallPeriodVaryMin, kFallPeriodVaryMax,
                                       hash21(cid + 29.7));
                float fallPeriod = (fallSpan / fallSpeed) * periodVary;
                float fallSpeedC = fallSpan / fallPeriod;               // = 2E/T
                float cellPhase  = hash21(cid + 5.3) * fallPeriod;

                // The ripple centre is this cell's jittered drop column — the
                // SAME world XY the streak comes down at (drop_column_xy) — PLUS
                // the wind drift at impact (added below once impactT is known).
                float2 jitter = hash22(cid * 1.37 + 3.1);
                float2 columnXY = (cid + jitter) * kRippleSpacing;

                // The drop physically reaches groundZ only when groundZ lies in
                // its fall band (dropTopZ - 2E, dropTopZ]. Outside that band the
                // drop never lands here, so no ripple (avoids spurious impacts
                // on ground far below the camera volume).
                if (groundZ <= dropTopZ - fallSpan || groundZ > dropTopZ) {
                    continue;
                }

                // ── Impact-driven timing (mirrors drop_impact_time) ──
                // Most-recent time <= now at which the drop crossed groundZ:
                //   tau     = (dropTopZ - groundZ) / fallSpeedC ∈ [0, fallPeriod)
                //   impactT = cellPhase + tau + floor((t - base)/T) * T
                float tau     = (dropTopZ - groundZ) / fallSpeedC;
                float base    = cellPhase + tau;
                float k       = floor((materialParams.time - base) / fallPeriod);
                float impactT = base + k * fallPeriod;

                // ── WIND-shifted ripple centre (mirrors drop_xy_at_z at
                //    z = groundZ with windStrength(impactT)) ──
                // The drop drifts kWindDir * windStrength * (dropTopZ - groundZ)
                // horizontally by the time it reaches the ground; using the
                // strength at IMPACT places the centre where the drop actually
                // was when it hit. The streak shader applies the identical
                // offset to its landing point, so they coincide. At windStrength
                // 0 this is exactly the column.
                float windStrengthAtImpact = kWindStrengthBase
                    + kWindStrengthAmp * sin(impactT * kWindGustFreq);
                float2 centreWorldXY = columnXY
                    + kWindDir * windStrengthAtImpact * (dropTopZ - groundZ);

                // Ripple animates over [impactT, impactT + rippleDuration). The
                // duration is per-cell, hard-clamped <= 2.0 s. Outside the
                // window this cell contributes nothing.
                float rippleDur = min(mix(rippleDurMin, rippleDurMax,
                                          hash21(cid + 91.7)), kRippleDurCap);
                float rippleAge = materialParams.time - impactT;
                if (rippleAge < 0.0 || rippleAge >= rippleDur) {
                    continue;
                }
                float t01 = rippleAge / rippleDur;

                // World-metre radius from this cell's ripple centre.
                float r = length(worldAbs.xy - centreWorldXY);

                // Per-cell alpha scales the whole contribution (size already
                // varied via cellMaxRadius / cellRingWidth).
                ring += rippleRing(r, t01, cellMaxRadius, cellRingWidth) * cellAlpha;

                // Faint, brief central birth sparkle. LOOK PASS: a weak (0.3),
                // tight (0.008 Gaussian), brief (first ~12% of life) pin sparkle
                // at the moment of impact, scaled by the per-cell alpha so faint
                // impacts have a faint flash too.
                core += materialParams.splashShadingTuning.y
                      * exp(-r * r /
                            (materialParams.splashShapeTuning.w *
                             kRippleSpacing * kRippleSpacing))
                      * (1.0 - smoothstep(0.0, 0.12, t01))
                      * cellAlpha;
            }
        }

        float profile = ring + core;

        if (profile <= 0.0) {
            material.baseColor = float4(0.0);
            return;
        }

        // ── Composite: watery translucent highlight ──
        float alpha = profile * intensity * distFade * kAlphaGain;
        alpha = clamp(alpha, 0.0, 1.0);

        // Premultiplied alpha for Filament's `transparent` blender (same
        // convention as heaven_volumetric_fog / heaven_unlit / heaven_smoke).
        material.baseColor = float4(kRippleColor * alpha, alpha);
    }
}
